  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Until now, adjectives always came at the end of the sentence: ケーキは おいしいです (The cake is delicious). This time the adjective moves to the front of a noun and describes it directly: おいしい ケーキ (delicious cake). The adjective and the noun now work as one block, and that block can go anywhere a plain noun can go: before です, before は or も, or after の. い-adjectives go straight in front of the noun with no change. な-adjectives need a な between the adjective and the noun: しずかな みせ (a quiet shop).",
    "structure_notes": [
      "Japanese puts the describing word first, like English: [adjective] [noun]. おいしい コーヒー = delicious coffee, たのしい よてい = fun plan.",
      "い-adjectives attach directly: つめたい ケーキ, あつい コーヒー. Don't add anything in between.",
      "な-adjectives need な before the noun: しずか → しずかな みせ, きれい → きれいな かさ. Leaving out the な is a common beginner mistake.",
      "The [adjective + noun] block is still just a noun phrase. It takes the same slots a noun does: 'たのしい よてい です', 'あつい コーヒー も どうぞ', 'しずかな みせ の コーヒー'.",
      "Compare the two positions. 'ケーキ は おいしい です' is a statement about the cake (the adjective comes at the end). 'おいしい ケーキ です' just names the thing (the adjective sits inside the noun phrase)."
    ],
    "connects_to": "In episodes 8 to 10 you used い- and な-adjectives at the end of a sentence with です. In this episode the same adjectives move in front of a noun, and な-adjectives get a な on the end when they do. The noun phrase they form fits into patterns you already know, like 〜は〜です, 〜も and 〜の〜."
  },
